/* Learning content per topic: plain/engineer explanations, quiz questions, tutor suggestions and the glossary. */
export const LV={
 beyond:['Most computers keep their data in one place and do the math in another, and the trip between the two now costs far more energy than the math. New kinds of computers try to shorten that trip, use physics directly, or tackle problems ordinary logic handles badly. None wins everywhere: each trades flexibility or precision for speed or energy on certain jobs.','At 45 nm a 16-bit DRAM access costs about 640 pJ against 0.18 pJ for an add; on-chip SRAM is 8–11 pJ. Paradigms differ in which term they attack: data movement (compute-in-memory, dataflow and 3D), switching energy (adiabatic and reversible, superconducting), or algorithmic class (quantum for structured problems, probabilistic for sampling and optimization). Evaluate each by energy-to-solution on a fixed workload, precision, programmability and maturity, not peak operations per second.'],
 everywhere:['Almost everything with a battery or a plug has chips inside, and most of them are not the famous processors. A phone or a car holds dozens of different chips: some think, some remember, some handle power, some talk by radio and some sense the world. Most are made in older factories, because they need to be cheap and reliable more than they need to be tiny.','Products partition functions across a fast application SoC on a leading-edge node and a long tail of mature-node parts: PMICs and drivers in BCD processes, RF front ends in GaAs and RF-SOI, MEMS sensors, embedded-flash MCUs at 28–90 nm, and memory on DRAM and NAND processes. Node choice is set by cost per function, analog and high-voltage needs, embedded non-volatile memory, qualification (AEC-Q100 for cars) and supply longevity. The flexibility–efficiency spectrum (CPU → GPU/DSP → FPGA → ASIC) spans two to three orders of magnitude in energy per operation for fixed workloads.'],
 architecture:['Think of an instruction set as a language and a chip as someone who speaks it. x86, Arm and RISC-V are three languages. Software written in one runs on any chip that speaks it, whether that chip is fast or slow. Pipelining is an assembly line: while one instruction is being finished, the next ones are already being started.','High-performance cores decode 6 to 10 instructions per cycle into micro-operations, rename registers to remove false dependencies, and execute out of order from windows of several hundred instructions behind branch predictors that are right well over 95% of the time. x86 pays a decode cost for variable-length instructions, usually hidden by a micro-op cache; fixed-length Arm and RISC-V decode in parallel more easily. Today the ISA matters less for raw speed than for ecosystem, licensing and extensions such as Arm SME, RISC-V vector and matrix extensions, and x86 AVX10 and AMX.'],
 design:['Designing a chip is like writing a huge program that turns into a city plan. Engineers write code that describes circuits, software turns it into billions of tiny switches, and checking that everything works takes longer than writing it. Every choice trades speed, power and size against each other.','Static timing analysis checks setup and hold on every path across process, voltage and temperature corners with on-chip variation derates. Power sign-off covers dynamic power (αCV²f), leakage and IR drop, managed with clock gating, multiple voltage domains and dynamic voltage and frequency scaling. Verification combines UVM constrained-random simulation, formal property checking and hardware emulation, tracked by code and functional coverage.'],
 layout:['Placement is like arranging seats at a huge dinner so people who talk the most sit closest. Shorter wires mean faster signals and less wasted power.','Production placers use analytical global placement (smoothed wirelength models such as log-sum-exp or weighted-average plus a density penalty, as in ePlace and RePlAce), then legalization and detailed placement, with timing- and congestion-driven weights. Buffering and clock-tree synthesis follow. Backside power delivery on TSMC A16 and Intel 18A frees front-side routing tracks and changes how standard cells are designed.'],
 physics:['Silicon is a material that conducts electricity only a little. Adding a pinch of other atoms decides whether it carries negative or positive charges, and a voltage on a nearby gate can switch it between conducting and not. Everything starts with a crystal so perfect that every atom is in its place.','Intrinsic carrier density n_i ≈ 10¹⁰ cm⁻³ at 300 K follows exp(−E_g/2kT); doping from 10¹⁵ to 10²⁰ cm⁻³ sets majority carriers with n·p = n_i². Junction physics: V_bi = (kT/q)·ln(N_A·N_D/n_i²), depletion width ∝ √((V_bi − V)/N). Czochralski growth uses Dash necking to eliminate dislocations, with diameter controlled by pull rate and melt temperature; oxygen from the quartz crucible (~10¹⁸ cm⁻³) aids gettering. Implant profiles follow projected range R_p and straggle ΔR_p; spike, flash and laser anneals trade activation against diffusion.'],
 transistor:['A transistor is a tap for electricity, and the gate is the handle. As taps got tiny, they started dripping even when closed. Each new design grips the pipe from more sides so it shuts off tightly.','Subthreshold swing is (kT/q)·ln10·(1 + C_dep/C_ox), which has a floor of about 60 mV per decade at room temperature. Short-channel effects appear as drain-induced barrier lowering and threshold roll-off; wrapping the gate around the channel restores electrostatic control so gate length can shrink with acceptable leakage. Nanosheet width can vary by cell to trade drive current for area, and the forksheet adds a dielectric wall between n and p sheets on the way to CFET.'],
 litho:['Lithography is printing with light through a stencil that is shrunk four times. When lines get thinner than the light can draw, engineers switch to shorter light (EUV), bigger lenses, cleverly angled lighting, or split the pattern across two stencils.','Half-pitch resolution is k₁λ/NA, with practical single-exposure k₁ down to about 0.25 using off-axis illumination; depth of focus scales as λ/NA², so High-NA trades focus margin for resolution. High-NA uses anamorphic 4×/8× reduction, which halves the exposure field to 26 × 16.5 mm. EUV stochastics (photon shot noise, secondary electrons, resist chemistry) push dose up and set line-edge roughness and missing-contact rates.'],
 fab:['A fab is a giant kitchen that repeats one recipe hundreds of times: add a layer, print a pattern, carve it, fill it, polish it, check it. It must be far cleaner than a hospital and more precise than almost anything else people manufacture.','Process control relies on statistical process control, run-to-run feedback and fault detection on every chamber. Key loops include per-field lithography corrections for dimensions and overlay using high-order wafer and field models, etch endpoint detection, ALD saturation curves, and CMP removal following Preston\'s law (rate proportional to pressure times velocity) with dummy fill to limit dishing. Cycle time is roughly 1 to 1.5 days per mask layer.'],
 yield:['Picture raindrops falling on a tiled floor: any tile hit by a drop is ruined. Big tiles get hit more often, so cutting a design into smaller tiles (chiplets) wastes less.','Common yield models are Poisson e^(−AD₀), Murphy ((1 − e^(−AD₀)) ÷ AD₀)² and negative binomial (1 + AD₀/α)^(−α), where α captures how defects cluster. Defect density falls along a learning curve as a node ramps. Redundancy (spare memory rows, fused-off cores) and binning recover partly defective dies, which is why many products ship with some units disabled.'],
 package:['Packaging used to be just a protective case. Now it is like building a tiny city block: processor buildings next to memory towers, joined by extremely dense roads so data never travels far.','CoWoS-S uses one silicon interposer; CoWoS-L embeds small silicon bridges in an organic redistribution layer to go beyond the reticle size. Micro-bump pitches are tens of micrometres while hybrid bonding goes below 10 µm. HBM4 doubles the interface to 2,048 bits, and with a foundry-made base die, customers can put custom logic under the memory stack. Heat density, warpage and testing stacks before assembly are the main yield challenges.'],
 compute:['An AI chip is a giant multiplication machine. It runs fastest when its thousands of calculators stay busy, which means feeding them data quickly from memory and serving many people at once.','Decode is memory-bound: arithmetic intensity is about 2 × batch ÷ bytes per weight FLOP per byte, far below ridge points in the thousands. KV-cache reads add to weight traffic at long context. Standard tools include FP8 and FP4 quantization with block scaling, speculative decoding, continuous batching, paged KV caches, and splitting compute-bound prefill from bandwidth-bound decode onto different hardware, which NVIDIA\'s Rubin CPX targets.'],
 accelerators:['All AI chips are giant calculators. The big difference is where they keep the numbers they work on: in memory chips next door (GPUs and TPUs), or spread through the chip itself (wafer-scale and SRAM designs). Keeping numbers closer is faster, but holds less.','The design space has five main axes: compute density (MACs per mm²), memory hierarchy (HBM capacity versus on-chip SRAM bandwidth), programmability (SIMT cores versus compiler-scheduled systolic or dataflow execution), scale-up fabric (NVLink, ICI, UALink) and number formats (FP8 E4M3/E5M2, block-scaled MXFP4 and NVFP4). Decode rewards bandwidth per byte of model; prefill and training reward dense FLOPS and fabric bandwidth.'],
 datacenter:['An AI chip is like a giant factory that needs constant deliveries. Memory chips are the warehouses next door, the network is the road system between factories, and the power plant keeps the lights on. If any of them can\'t keep up, the factory sits idle.','DRAM retention scales roughly 2× per 10 °C, so refresh (tREFI 3.9 µs, tRFC hundreds of ns) costs a few percent of bandwidth. 3D NAND scales by layer count (286–400+ layers, multi-deck channel-hole etch, CMOS bonded to array) and bits per cell, trading endurance and read margin. HBM4 doubles the interface to 2,048 bits; stack height within 775 µm forces die thinning and pushes hybrid bonding for 16- to 20-high. Scale-up fabrics (NVLink 6 at 3.6 TB/s per GPU) versus scale-out at 1.6 Tb/s per GPU shape parallelism; optics power and PUE shape joules per token.'],
 ecosystem:['Making a chip is like making a film: one studio gets the credit, but it depends on writers, camera makers, sound studios and cinemas. Some of those suppliers are the only ones in the world who can do their part.','Chokepoints form where learning curves and capital needs are steepest: EUV scanners (ASML), leading-edge foundry (TSMC), EDA (three vendors), HBM (three suppliers), photoresists and some specialty gases. Export controls target exactly these points. Concentration also sets pricing power and how quickly new entrants, such as Rapidus, or new business models, such as Arm selling its own chip, can change the structure.'],
 supply:['Making a chip is like a relay race run across a dozen countries: designers in California, toolmakers in the Netherlands and Japan, factories in Taiwan, memory in Korea. If any runner stops, the whole race stops, and some runners have no substitute.','Concentration follows economies of scale and learning: leading-edge logic in Taiwan, HBM in Korea, EUV in the Netherlands, resists, wafers and ABF film in Japan, gallium and rare-earth refining in China. Resilience levers are inventory (weeks to months), multi-sourcing (requires 6–12 months of requalification per product and node) and geographic diversification (years, and higher cost per wafer). Export controls weaponize the same chokepoints.'],
 economics:['A chip is expensive to invent but cheap to copy once the factory is running. So companies spend fortunes up front on design and factories, then try to sell as many chips as possible. Whoever controls a step nobody else can do keeps most of the profit.','Unit economics: die cost ≈ wafer price ÷ (gross dies × yield); package cost is dominated by HBM and CoWoS, with yield loss scrapping known-good components. Business-model margins reflect scarcity rents (NVIDIA ~75% gross, TSMC ~66%, SK hynix 76% operating at the 2026 HBM peak). Fixed costs scale steeply with node: mask sets of $15–25M and design NRE in the hundreds of millions at 3–2 nm, which pushes startups toward older nodes, shuttles, chiplets and licensed IP.'],
 innovation:['Some jobs get faster with better software. Some need a scientific breakthrough. Some need money and years to build a factory. Some need trained people and clear rules. AI directly speeds up only the first kind.','Treat the chain as a queue of constrained resources: compressing steps that are not the bottleneck (design time) raises pressure on the ones that are (fab and packaging capacity, power). Track CoWoS wafer starts, HBM bit supply, EUV tool shipments, grid connection queues and engineering headcount, not design productivity alone.'],
 future:['Chips used to get better mainly by shrinking. Now they improve by stacking, smarter packaging, faster memory and new materials. The roadmap is a best guess that gets revised every year.','Through 2030 most density gains come from shorter standard cells, backside power and CFET rather than gate-pitch scaling, which imec expects to stay near 42 nm from A10 to A5. Beyond that, 2D channels, Hyper-NA EUV and 3D integration are the main levers, while system-level gains come from co-designing packaging, memory and interconnect.'],
 scenarios:['Nobody knows exactly how chips will improve. Some bets, like new transistor shapes or enough electricity, could go well or badly, and each changes the result. Exploring several futures is more honest than drawing one line.','Model efficiency as a sum of log-rate contributions (process, architecture/numerics, packaging, design productivity), each decaying, and installed compute as the minimum of power-limited and supply-limited capacity. Under most assumptions power, not wafer supply, binds by the 2030s, which raises the value of joules per operation over raw FLOPS. Beyond-CMOS candidates are best tracked by evidence: peer-reviewed results, engineering samples and shipments.']
};
